 { When possessing separated passed pawns, a key idea is to abandon one of them in order to secure the advance of the other one, as in the present example. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf3 $1 { White heads at once for the opposite flank. } ( 1. Rb8 $2 Rxg4 2. Kf3 Rb4 { would let Black escape to a draw; e.g. } 3. b6 Kf6 4. b7 ( { or } 4. Ke3 Ke6 5. b7 Kd7 ) 
Kg7 $1 { with a draw. } ) 
Rf4+ ( { a) Now } 1... Rxg4 $2 { fails to } 2. Rg6 $1 Kxg6 3. Kxg4 ) 
 ( { b) While } 1... Rb3 { doesn't work either due to } 2. Ke4 Kxg4 3. Kd5 Kf5 4. Kc6 Ke6 5. Rb8 { and White wins with ease. Note that in endings with rook and pawn vs. rook, if the defending king can't reach its preferred location in front of the pawn, then the knight's pawn is particularly powerful, since there is no 'shorter side' available for the defending king (more on this subject in Chapter Three). } ) 
2. Ke3 Rxg4 3. Kd3 Kf5 4. Kc3 Ke5 5. Rc6 $1 { Cutting the enemy king off from the c-file. } ( { In contrast, } 5. Rb8 $2 { lets Black approach the king by } Kd6 $1 { as observed by fairnoelia. Here } 6. Rc8 { comes too late in view of } Kd7 7. Rc6 Rg8 $1 { (7...Rh4 in waiting fashion also works) } 8. Kb4 Rc8 { - on the topic of building a bridge - } 9. Rxc8 Kxc8 10. Ka5 Kb7 { etc. } ) 
Kd5 6. Rc8 ( { White wins quickly; e.g. } 6. Rc8 Rg6 7. Kb4 { followed by Ka5, b5-b6 etc. } ) 

