 { The further apart, the more powerful are separated passed pawns. Here White doesn't just possess two rook's pawns, he has also managed to advance them quite far, yet the win is not trivial. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf4 $1 { Zugzwang. } ( { a) More straightforward moves won't work; e.g. } 1. a6 $2 Bxa6 2. e6 Kg8 $1 3. e7 Bb5 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 1. e6 $2 Kxe6 2. a6 Kf7 $1 3. a7 Be4 4. h7 Kg7 { and Black has again managed to assign the defensive tasks in a correct way. } ) 
 ( { c) } 1. Kh5 $6 { doesn't make much sense after } Bc4 { , when White would have to revert to the main line by } 2. Kg5 { , getting ready to meet } Bd3 { with } 3. Kf4 $1 ) 
Bc4 ( { a) Nothing helps; e.g. } 1... Ke6 2. a6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 1... Kg6 2. e6 Kxh6 3. e7 Bb5 4. a6 Kg7 5. a7 ) 
2. Ke4 $1 ( 2. h7 $2 Kg7 { After 2.Ke4 Black's bishop proves overloaded, so he resigned. Let's make a few more moves: } ) 
Ba6 3. e6+ Kg6 4. e7 Bb5 5. a6 { White wins. } 
 1-0

