 { In exchange for the pawn deficit Black has the more active rook, though he should evidently be on his guard against a possible rook exchange. Hillarp found a clever idea. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { This passed pawn will soon prove useful. } ( { In contrast, approaching the king with } 1... Kf6 $6 { permits } 2. b5 { and the b-pawn becomes very dangerous. } ) 
2. Re3 ( { A tempting choice; but with hindsight White should probably try } 2. Kf3 { or } 2. b3 { instead. } ) 
Re5 $1 ( { Of course not } 2... Rxe3 $2 3. Kxe3 { when the protected passed pawn on b4 decides the game. } ) 
3. Rxe5+ ( { After } 3. Kf3 Kd7 { Black should also be able to draw. } ) 
dxe5 { Black's idea becomes clear: his separated passed pawns will keep the enemy king busy. } 4. b5 Kd6 5. b6 Kd7 $1 ( { The only move; since both } 5... e4 $2 6. Ke3 ) 
 ( { and } 5... g4 $2 6. Kg3 { would lose on the spot. } ) 
6. Kf3 Kd6 7. Kf2 Kd7 8. Kf3 Kd6 9. Ke3 ( { Or } 9. Kg3 e4 $1 { with similar play. } ) 
g4 $1 ( { Now the separated passed pawns mutually protect themselves, just like their white counterparts, by the way. A different move order with } 9... Kd7 10. Ke4 g4 $1 { also works. } ) 
10. Ke4 Kd7 11. Ke3 Kd6 12. Ke4 Kd7 13. Ke3 
 1/2-1/2

