 { Here is a final example, which shows once more that in pawn endings the separated passed pawns can often prove stronger than their connected counterparts. } 1... { -KEY- } Rd4 $2 ( { A tempting, but fatal choice. By means of the threat } 2... Rd2 { Black manages to 'fix' his pawn structure, but he has overlooked his opponent's active possibilities in the pawn ending that now arises. } ) 
 ( { Something like } 1... Rf4 2. Kg3 ( { or } 1... Rd4 1. Ke3 Rh4 ) 
Rf7 $1 { followed by ...e5-e4, ...Ke5 etc, was preferable. } ) 
2. Rxd4 exd4 3. b4 $1 ( { The key move, by which White prepares the creation of a second passed pawn by b4-b5. An alternative move order with } 3. h4 Kf5 4. b4 { also works. } ) 
 ( { However, } 3. a4 $2 { should be avoided in view of } a5 $1 { with a 'one pawn stops two' situation. } ) 
a6 ( { Or } 3... Kf5 4. h4 { with similar play. } ) 
4. h4 ( 4. a4 { first is also OK. } ) 
Kf5 5. a4 $1 ( 5. h5 { first also works. } ) 
Ke4 ( { Equally useless is } 5... e5 6. h5 Kg5 7. b5 { etc. } ) 
6. h5 ( 6. Ke2 $5 { is an even simpler way, when again White's separated passed pawns are too much to handle for Black's king, as observed by HorseyChess. } ) 
Kf5 ( { After } 6... Kd3 7. h6 Kc2 8. h7 d3 9. h8=Q $11 d2 10. Qh7 Kc1 { White can win both by } 11. Qh6 { approaching with the queen in staircase fashion, } ( { and } 10... Kf5 10. Qxe7 d1=Q $11 12. Qe1 $1 { followed by b4-b5 after exchanging queens - Hecht. } ) 
 ) 
7. h6 ( { An inverted move order with } 7. b5 { also works, for example, } cxb5 8. axb5 axb5 9. c6 Ke6 10. h6 { and only White queens. } ) 
Kg6 8. b5 
 1-0
